## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end, fault-tolerant data pipelines and reason about exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in real-time systems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing of streaming pipelines
- Idempotency and deduplication strategies
- Replay, backpressure, and regional outage resilience
- Metrics: data loss, duplicates, latency, SLAs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple dedup: track seen event_ids to skip duplicates in sinks
const seen = new Set();
function process(event){
  if(seen.has(event.event_id)) return false; // duplicate
  seen.add(event.event_id);
  // write to sink
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cross-region failover and data skew in this pipeline?
- What changes would you make if sinks cannot guarantee exactly-once delivery?
